   "title": "NNPC Plans 5%To 10% IPO Share Sale, Ojulari Tells Reuters",
   "url": "https://leadership.ng/nnpc-plans-5to-10-ipo-share-sale-ojulari-tells-reuters/",
   "domain": "leadership.ng",
   "source": "Leadership（尼日利亚）",
   "published": "Tue, 03 Nov 2026 17:21:31 +0000",
   "_dt": 1793726491.0,
   "ageDays": 1.62,
   "summary": "Ojulari told Reuters that the IPO would initially focus on the domestic market, although NNPC could later pursue a secondary listing in London or New York. The planned share sale would mark a major step for Nigeria’s oil sector, where NNPC has long faced calls for greater commercial discipline and financial openness.",
   "summary_zh": "Ojulari 告诉路透社，首次公开募股（IPO）将主要聚焦国内市场，但尼日利亚国家石油公司（NNPC）日后可能在伦敦或纽约进行二次上市。拟议中的股票出售将标志着尼日利亚石油行业迈出重要一步；长期以来，NNPC 一直面临加强商业纪律和财务公开透明度的呼声。",
   "summarySource": "fulltext",
   "body": "Ojulari told Reuters that the IPO would initially focus on the domestic market, although NNPC could later pursue a secondary listing in London or New York. The company may also consider listing some of its subsidiaries, following a model used by the United Arab Emirates’ state oil company, ADNOC. The CEO said preparations for a listing would likely take at least all of 2027. He said the main goal was to strengthen NNPC’s transparency and governance in line with Nigerian law, while raising capital would be a secondary objective. The planned share sale would mark a major step for Nigeria’s oil sector, where NNPC has long faced calls for greater commercial discipline and financial openness. A successful IPO could also broaden local investor participation in the country’s energy industry. Separately, Ojulari said Chinese companies had expressed interest in acquiring equity stakes—not merely contracts—in some of NNPC’s smaller refineries that require major repairs, including Port Harcourt and Warri. He said NNPC would retain at least 10% ownership under any such arrangement and expected at least one of the smaller refineries to resume production within three years. The development comes as Nigeria’s Dangote refinery recently attracted 6.4 trillion naira, or about $4.78 billion, in orders during its domestic IPO, underscoring strong investor appetite for Nigerian energy assets. We’ve got the edge. Get real-time reports, breaking scoops, and exclusive angles delivered straight to your phone. Don’t settle for stale news. Join LEADERSHIP NEWS on WhatsApp for 24/7 updates →",
   "bodyLevel": "fulltext",
